{
  "sections": {
    "science": {
      "type": "vb-science-page",
      "blocks": {
        "hea_43": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "kicker": "The problem nobody prints on the label",
            "title": "Probiotics die on the shelf"
          }
        },
        "tex_44": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "content": "<p>A live culture is exactly that: alive. From the day it is packed it slowly loses viability, and heat and humidity speed the loss up dramatically. Industry surveys have found that losses of 3 log, a thousand-fold, within 60 days of manufacture are common in dry probiotic products. That is why many brands print \"keep refrigerated\" and why the count on the label is usually the count on the day of manufacture, not the day you take it.</p><p>For most of South Africa, and much of the world, the cold chain is not guaranteed. Products sit in hot delivery vans, un-airconditioned stockrooms and bathroom cabinets. A probiotic that only works out of a fridge is not much use there.</p>"
          }
        },
        "fig_45": {
          "type": "figure",
          "settings": {
            "image_url": "https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0668/5848/3883/files/velobiotics-science-hero-heat.jpg?v=1790193717",
            "alt": "Probiotic bottles on a hot rural pharmacy shelf, far from refrigeration",
            "label": "Figure 1",
            "caption": "Real-world storage: a warm shelf, a long delivery route, no refrigeration. This is the environment the 30 °C test was designed to mimic.",
            "size": "wide"
          }
        },
        "hea_46": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "kicker": "The test",
            "title": "Twelve weeks at 30 °C"
          }
        },
        "tex_47": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "content": "<p>Researchers at the University of Pretoria and the <a class=\"vb-sci-link\" href=\"/pages/velobiotics-story\">CSIR</a> stored <a class=\"vb-sci-link\" href=\"/pages/what-is-microencapsulation\">micro-shield</a>ed and unprotected <em>Bifidobacterium longum</em> Bb46 and <em>Bifidobacterium lactis</em> Bb12 as dry powders in glass vials at 30 °C for 12 weeks, counting live cells every one to two weeks. Thirty degrees was chosen deliberately: it is an accelerated test, hotter than a normal room, so that a year of ordinary shelf life plays out in a few months.</p><p>The benchmark was the widely used therapeutic minimum of 10⁶ live cells per gram (6 log). Below that, a probiotic is generally considered to have too few live cells to be effective.</p>"
          }
        },
        "fig_48": {
          "type": "figure",
          "settings": {
            "image_url": "https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0668/5848/3883/files/velobiotics-science-chart-shelf-life-30c.png?v=1790180516",
            "alt": "Chart: at 30 °C microencapsulated bifidobacteria stay above the therapeutic minimum for 10 to 12 weeks versus 5 to 6 weeks unprotected",
            "label": "Figure 2",
            "caption": "Left: weeks each sample stayed above the therapeutic minimum at 30 °C. Right: total loss of live cells over 12 weeks (lower is better). Source: Thantsha, Labuschagne & Mamvura, World Journal of Microbiology and Biotechnology, 2014.",
            "size": "wide"
          }
        },
        "hea_49": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "kicker": "The result",
            "title": "Weeks more life, a million times more cells"
          }
        },
        "tex_50": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "content": "<p><strong><em>B. longum</em> Bb46</strong> is the more delicate of the two species, and it showed the biggest benefit. Unprotected cells dropped below the therapeutic minimum after six weeks and were completely gone by week ten. Shielded cells stayed above the minimum for ten weeks (production sample) to twelve weeks (reactor sample). Over the whole period, unprotected cells lost 11 log of viability; shielded cells lost 5 log. Put differently, after 12 weeks of heat there were about a <strong>million times more</strong> live shielded bacteria than unprotected ones.</p><p><strong><em>B. lactis</em> Bb12</strong> is naturally hardier, and it still benefited: unprotected cells fell below the minimum at week five, while shielded cells stayed above it for the full twelve weeks. The final difference was 3.3 log, about two thousand times more live cells.</p>"
          }
        },
        "sta_51": {
          "type": "stats",
          "settings": {
            "v1": "6 → 10–12 wks",
            "l1": "B. longum: weeks above the therapeutic minimum, unprotected vs shielded",
            "v2": "5 → 12 wks",
            "l2": "B. lactis: weeks above the therapeutic minimum, unprotected vs shielded",
            "v3": "−11 vs −5 log",
            "l3": "B. longum: total loss over 12 weeks, unprotected vs shielded"
          }
        },
        "fig_52": {
          "type": "figure",
          "settings": {
            "image_url": "https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0668/5848/3883/files/velobiotics-science-chart-log-scale.png?v=1790180516",
            "alt": "Infographic: what a log reduction means, each log is a tenfold change",
            "label": "Figure 3",
            "caption": "Reading the numbers: scientists count bacteria in logs. Each log is a factor of ten, so a 6-log difference is a million-fold.",
            "size": "normal"
          }
        },
        "hea_53": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "kicker": "Why it works",
            "title": "Keeping the water out"
          }
        },
        "tex_54": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "content": "<p>Water is the enemy of a dormant bacterium. A little humidity wakes it up; awake, it consumes what little food it has and dies. The Velobiotics™ shield is a hydrogen-bonded mesh of two polymers that holds noticeably less water than the same polymers loosely mixed, about 30% less in the CSIR's humidity tests. Throughout the 12-week study the water activity of the powders stayed between 0.25 and 0.43, and the periods of highest water activity coincided with the biggest losses in every sample, shielded or not. The shield does not make the bacteria immortal; it slows the clock dramatically.</p>"
          }
        },
        "fig_55": {
          "type": "figure",
          "settings": {
            "image_url": "https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0668/5848/3883/files/velobiotics-science-chart-moisture.png?v=1790180515",
            "alt": "Chart: polymers bonded in supercritical CO2 absorb 30% less moisture than simple powder blends",
            "label": "Figure 4",
            "caption": "Moisture absorbed after 72 hours at 60% humidity and 30 °C. Bonding the polymers in supercritical CO₂ cuts water uptake in every system tested. Source: Moolman et al., 2006, Table 2.",
            "size": "normal"
          }
        },
        "quo_56": {
          "type": "quote",
          "settings": {
            "text": "This method of encapsulation reveals the possibility for manufacture of encapsulated probiotic powders with increased stability at ambient temperatures. This would potentially allow the supply of a stable probiotic formulation to impoverished communities without proper storage facilities.",
            "cite": "Thantsha, Labuschagne & Mamvura, World Journal of Microbiology and Biotechnology, 2014"
          }
        },
        "cal_57": {
          "type": "callout",
          "settings": {
            "label": "What this means for you:",
            "text": "Velobiotics™ products are formulated for real South African conditions. Keep them cool and dry like any supplement, but you do not need a fridge, and the count on the label means more because far more of it is still alive when you take it.",
            "tone": "green"
          }
        },
        "det_58": {
          "type": "details",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Storage conditions and measurement",
            "content": "<p>Samples were stored as free powders in glass vials at 30 ± 2 °C for 12 weeks. Sub-samples were taken every 7 or 14 days for water activity (a<sub>w</sub>) and viable counts, plated in triplicate on MRS agar with 0.05% cysteine hydrochloride and incubated anaerobically. Water activities across all samples ranged 0.25–0.43 (mean 0.34). Each reported value is the mean of triplicate counts from three independent experiments.</p>"
          }
        },
        "det_59": {
          "type": "details",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Full results by species",
            "content": "<p><strong><em>B. longum</em> Bb46.</strong> Unprotected: −6 log after 8 weeks, no viable cells at 10 weeks, above 6 log CFU/g only until week 6, total reduction 11 log at 12 weeks. Shielded (product chamber): above 6 log for 10 weeks, −4 log at 8 weeks, total reduction 5 log. Shielded (reactor-harvested): above 6 log for 12 weeks, −3 log at 8 weeks. Reactor samples were significantly higher than unprotected (p < 0.05); product-chamber samples somewhat higher (p = 0.05).</p><p><strong><em>B. lactis</em> Bb12.</strong> Shielded cells lost 5 log over 12 weeks; unprotected cells dry-mixed with unprocessed and processed polymers lost 6 and 8 log respectively (−8.2 log at the end point vs −4.9 log shielded). Unprotected samples fell below 6 log CFU/g by week 5; shielded stayed above 6 log to week 12. The largest losses for every sample occurred in weeks 3–5, coinciding with the highest water activities.</p>"
          }
        },
        "det_60": {
          "type": "details",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How this compares with other encapsulation methods",
            "content": "<p>The authors compared their results with published alginate, gelatin and protein-carbohydrate encapsulation studies, which typically report survival gains of 0.5 to 2 log over 2 to 12 weeks, mostly under refrigeration. The 6-log gain for <em>B. longum</em> at 30 °C is among the largest reported. The authors also noted that shear and heat during atomisation slightly reduced viability compared with reactor-harvested material, and that the product-chamber material was used for further work because it reflects practical large-scale production.</p>"
          }
        },
        "det_61": {
          "type": "details",
          "settings": {
            "title": "References",
            "content": "<ol><li>Thantsha MS, Labuschagne PW, Mamvura CI. Supercritical CO₂ interpolymer complex encapsulation improves heat stability of probiotic bifidobacteria. <em>World Journal of Microbiology and Biotechnology</em> 2014;30:479–486. <a href=\"https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0668/5848/3883/files/Velobiotics-Research-scCO2-Encapsulation-Heat-Stability-Shelf-Life-WJMB-2014.pdf?v=1789280831\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF</a></li><li>Moolman FS et al. Encapsulating probiotics with an interpolymer complex in supercritical carbon dioxide. <em>South African Journal of Science</em> 2006;102:349–354 (moisture absorption data). <a href=\"https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0668/5848/3883/files/Velobiotics-Research-Encapsulating-Probiotics-Interpolymer-Complex-scCO2-SAJS-2006.pdf?v=1789280831\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF</a></li></ol>"
          }
        }
      },
